WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.384
In the next domain, we are going to cover the OSI model and talk about layers on the OSI model.

00:06.384 --> 00:15.675
In relation to that, there are two types of switches we can use: layer 2 and layer 3 switches. Earlier I mentioned that switches use MAC addresses to control traffic.

00:15.675 --> 00:38.077
Any switch I connect to will learn this MAC address and use it to send traffic to this device. That is a layer 2 switch. What switches do not do is route traffic between networks.

00:38.077 --> 00:42.785
For this, you need a router, or a layer 3 switch, also known as a multilayer switch.

00:42.785 --> 00:49.681
That type of switch can use IP addresses, the logical address of this device, to route traffic between networks.

00:49.681 --> 01:00.003
That's the major difference between a layer 2 and a layer 3 switch. For the exam, you also just need to be aware of security options for a switch.

01:00.003 --> 01:06.322
Basically, switches use MAC tables to learn MAC addresses of devices connected to the switch.

01:06.322 --> 01:15.426
In most cases, if no traffic is generated on a connection, these entries will erase themselves from the MAC table in due time.

01:15.426 --> 01:21.094
One thing you can control on a managed switch is the number of addresses each switch port will memorize.

01:21.094 --> 01:31.279
If, for example, you only allow one address per port, this will help prevent someone from connecting a rogue device to the network and sending traffic through the switch,

01:31.279 --> 01:40.204
because that switch will realize, Hey, you're not the MAC address I have down for you on this port, and will not allow that traffic.

